  "worked_solution": "To find the second-degree Taylor polynomial about $x=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we plug $a=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ into the general formula to give\n\n$$\n{P}_{2}(x)=f(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+{f}^{\u2032}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\frac{1}{2}{f}^{\u2033}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}){(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we need to work out $f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ ${f}^{\u2032}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ and ${f}^{\u2033}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}):$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\operatorname{tan}\u2061(x) & \u27f9 &  & f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =\\frac{3}{2} \\\\ {f}^{\u2032}(x) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{{cos}^{2}\u2061(x)} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2032}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =3 \\\\ {f}^{\u2033}(x) & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(x)}{{cos}^{3}\u2061(x)} & \u27f9 &  & {f}^{\u2033}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now plug the results for $f(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ ${f}^{\u2032}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}),$ and ${f}^{\u2033}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ into our second-degree Taylor polynomial. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{2}(x) & =\\frac{3}{2}+3(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+6\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}+3(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})+3{(x-\\frac{\\pi }{4})}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
